---
/* THE CONTROL ON A VOLUME ROW: what you have searched, and where to start again.

   Draws itself into every `<li data-vol="...">` that has a `.volmark` slot,
   so a page only has to say WHICH rows are volumes and this decides what the
   control looks like. Requires ProgressKit.astro on the page.

   SHUT UNTIL IT HAS SOMETHING TO SAY. An unmarked volume shows one quiet
   link — «mark pages» — because a list of forty registers with forty open
   forms in it is unreadable, and most rows on most visits are not the one
   being worked on. A marked volume shows its ranges and its resume point
   without being asked, because that is the thing the reader came back for.
*/
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<script is:inline src={u("/js/volume-marks.js")}></script>

<style is:global>
  li[data-vol] .volmark { display: inline-flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px;
    align-items: center; margin-left: 0; margin-right: 10px; font-family: var(--sans);
    font-size: 11.5px; color: var(--ink-3); }
  /* When the editor is open the slot stops being an inline afterthought and
     becomes a block, so the panel gets the width it needs. */
  li[data-vol] .volmark:has(.vm-edit) { display: block; margin-left: 0; }
  /* QUIET BY COLOUR, NOT BY OPACITY. The first version dimmed this link with
     `opacity: .55` so forty unmarked rows would not shout, and put 25
     colour-contrast violations on every place page: opacity multiplies against
     the background and takes the ratio down with it, which axe measures and a
     reader with low vision feels. --ink-3 is pinned in this project's own
     stylesheet «by contrast, not by taste» and is the right tool — it is quiet
     AND it passes. The accent comes back on hover and on a marked row, where
     the link is the thing being looked for. */
  /* THE GREEN IS THE ONE THE SITE ALREADY SPEAKS, AT A VALUE THAT PASSES.
     #1E8449 is the access dot for «images, free, no account», and it is the
     obvious colour to reach for — but measured it is 3.66:1 on the paper
     ground and 3.46:1 on the dark card, which is large-text territory and
     this glyph is 13px. So the dot's green stays a dot, and the pill takes
     the nearest value that actually clears 4.5:1 on both halves of the site:
     #176B3A on paper (5.10) and #4FB477 on the dark card (6.32).
     Three blocks, the same three the token file uses and for the same reason
     — the system default, the OS preference scoped so an explicit light
     choice still wins, and the explicit dark choice. */
  /* THREE STATES, THREE TONES. Nothing recorded is quiet — it is the resting
     state of most rows on most visits and must not shout. Started is --brass,
     which is this project's own amber and the one colour here that has been
     contrast-corrected twice against this exact ground (4.84:1 on the paper,
     5.53:1 on the panel); inventing a fourth amber would be inventing a
     fourth thing to get wrong. Finished is the green. */
  .vm-pill { --walked: #6E6047; }
  .vm-pill-part { --walked: #7E5A20; }
  .vm-pill-on { --walked: #176B3A; }
  @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
    :root:not([data-theme="light"]) .vm-pill-on { --walked: #4FB477; }
    :root:not([data-theme="light"]) .vm-pill-part { --walked: #D9A441; }
  }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .vm-pill-on { --walked: #4FB477; }
  :root[data-theme="dark"] .vm-pill-part { --walked: #D9A441; }

  /* A TARGET BEFORE IT IS A DECORATION. 26px square is the smallest thing
     this project is willing to ask a thumb to hit, and the glyph is centred
     rather than sitting on the text baseline it no longer shares. */
  /* A CIRCLE, AND THE SAME CIRCLE IN EVERY STATE. David: «clear tick and
     filled tick are not the same, why is the one oval and not round?» Because
     the box was `min-width: 26px` plus 7px of horizontal padding, so its width
     followed its glyph — and only the filled state had been given a smaller
     font size, so the outlined tick was still drawing at the +'s 19px and
     stretched its own button into an oval.
     Fixed 26×26 with no padding: the shape is the shape whatever is inside
     it, and the glyph size is set per state below without moving anything. */
  /* 24px, MATCHING THE NUMBERED CIRCLES IN THE VISITING PANE — see the note
     beside .ra-ck-i in RecordMap.astro. 24 is WCAG 2.2's minimum target size,
     so this is the smallest the button is allowed to be and still be one. */
  .vm-pill { display: inline-flex; align-items: center;
    justify-content: center; width: 24px; height: 24px; padding: 0;
    flex: 0 0 24px; box-sizing: border-box;
    border: 1px solid var(--walked); border-radius: 50%; cursor: pointer;
    background: none; color: var(--walked); font-family: var(--sans);
    /* THE GLYPH IS THE WHOLE CONTROL, so it gets the weight the words used to
       have. At 14px regular the + read as a hairline crossing a circle and
       the circle looked empty at a glance — which is the one thing this pill
       must not do, because «is there anything here» is the question the list
       is scanned for. 19px at 700 fills the disc without touching its edge.
       `line-height: 1` with the flex centring above keeps it optically
       centred: a + carries top bearing that the normal line box would push
       down, and at this size that misalignment is visible. */
    font-size: 18px; font-weight: 700; line-height: 1; }
  .vm-pill:hover { background: var(--walked); color: var(--panel); }
  /* MARKED READS AS FILLED, so a list of forty answers «which of these have I
     done» without being read.
     --panel, AND NOT --card. --card is the estate token file's name for this
     colour and it is not defined on these pages: `var(--card)` was invalid at
     computed-value time, the declaration was dropped, and the tick inherited
     --ink-3 — muted brown on dark green, which is how it went out looking
     like an empty green disc. Measured after the fix, --panel is also the
     right choice rather than merely a working one: it flips with the theme
     exactly as --walked does, so it is a light glyph on the dark green by
     day (5.82:1) and a dark glyph on the light green at night (6.32:1). */
  /* THE TICK IS ALREADY A HEAVY SHAPE — matching the +'s 19px would make the
     done rows shout over the ones still to do, which is backwards. Same
     weight, smaller body, so the two read as one family at one glance. */
  /* BOTH TICKS AT ONE SIZE — the + is a wider, airier glyph and carries 19px
     comfortably; a ✓ at 19px fills the disc to its edge. */
  .vm-pill-part, .vm-pill-on { font-size: 14px; }
  .vm-pill-on { background: var(--walked); color: var(--panel);
    font-weight: 700; }
  .vm-pill:focus-visible { outline: 2px solid var(--focus);
    outline-offset: 2px; }
  /* NO LEFT RULE ANY MORE. David: «why do we have vertical lines next to some
     of our sources and not others?» — because a marked row used to carry one,
     from before the pill existed and said the same thing better. Two signals
     for one state, and the weaker of them looked like a bracket grouping the
     rows it happened to sit beside rather than a property of one row. The
     pill is the state. */
  /* STARTED IS A RING, FINISHED IS A DISC — so the two are told apart by fill
     as well as by hue, which is the part a colour-blind reader relies on. */
  .vm-pill-part { background: none; color: #7E5A20; border-color: #7E5A20; }
  .vm-detail { flex-basis: 100%; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 12px;
    color: var(--ink-2); }
  .vm-detail b { font-family: var(--mono); }
  li[data-vol] .volmark b { font-family: var(--mono); color: var(--ink-2); }
  .vm-bar { display: inline-block; width: 64px; height: 6px; border-radius: 3px;
    background: var(--rule); overflow: hidden; vertical-align: middle; }
  .vm-bar i { display: block; height: 100%; background: var(--accent); }
  .vm-res { font-family: var(--mono); color: var(--brick); }
  .vm-note { font-style: italic; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .vm-since { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10px; color: var(--ink-3); }
  /* A PANEL ON ITS OWN LINE, NOT A WHISPER AT THE END OF ONE. David clicked
     «mark pages» and reported that nothing happened. It had happened: the
     editor opened as an 11.5px inline strip at the end of a row that already
     carried a title, a year span, «Roman Catholic» and a film number, several
     hundred pixels right of where the click landed. A control nobody can see
     working is a control that does not work.
     Below the row, boxed, with room — so the click has an obvious result and
     the fields are big enough to type a page range into on a phone. */
  /* A COLUMN, NOT A WRAPPING LINE. See the note at editor() — the old rule
     was `flex-wrap: wrap` over every field at once, which is a layout that
     rearranges itself depending on the width of whatever list it landed in.
     A column of rows looks the same in a narrow panel and a wide page, which
     is the whole requirement for a form somebody types into. */
  .vm-edit { display: flex; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 10px; flex-basis: 100%; margin: 6px 0 4px; padding: 12px 14px;
    border: 1px solid var(--accent); border-left-width: 3px; border-radius: 4px;
    background: var(--ground); max-width: 34rem; }
  .vm-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 10px 14px; align-items: flex-end; }
  .vm-btns { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; margin-top: 2px; }
  /* Label above field, not beside it: «PAGES DONE» and «OF» beside their
     boxes made two columns of different widths that never lined up. */
  .vm-edit label { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 4px;
    align-items: flex-start;
    font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10px; text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: .05em; color: var(--ink-3); }
  .vm-edit input { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 14px; padding: 6px 9px;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); border-radius: 3px;
    background: var(--panel); color: var(--ink); width: 12em; }
  .vm-edit .vm-t { width: 5em; }
  /* The walked checkbox reads as a sentence, not as a field: it is the one
     control here that changes who the work belongs to, so it gets the full
     width of the editor and normal case rather than the uppercase mono the
     field labels wear. Without the width reset it would inherit `12em` from
     the rule above and draw a checkbox the size of a text box. */
  .vm-edit .vm-walk { flex-direction: row; align-items: center; gap: 7px;
    text-transform: none; font-size: 12px;
    font-family: var(--sans); letter-spacing: 0; color: var(--ink-2); }
  .vm-edit .vm-notel input { width: 20em; max-width: 100%; }
  .vm-edit .vm-w { width: auto; padding: 0; margin: 0; }
  .vm-edit button { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 13px; padding: 6px 13px;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong); border-radius: 3px;
    background: var(--panel); color: var(--ink); cursor: pointer; }
  .vm-edit .vm-save { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
  .vm-edit .vm-clear { color: var(--brick); border-color: var(--brick); }
  .srcmark { display: inline-flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px; align-items: baseline;
    margin-left: 8px; }
  .srcmark select, .srcmark input { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11.5px;
    padding: 2px 5px; border: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 3px;
    background: var(--panel); color: var(--ink); cursor: pointer; }
  .src-edit { display: inline-flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 5px;
    align-items: center; }
  .src-shut { background: none; border: 0; padding: 0 4px; cursor: pointer;
    font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1;
    color: var(--ink-3); }
  .src-shut:hover { color: var(--brick); }
  /* THE STATE IN WORDS, once there is one. The pill says «something is
     recorded here»; this says what, which is the thing a reader came back to
     find out and the reason the select is not simply left on the row. */
  .src-state { font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11.5px;
    color: var(--walked, var(--ink-2)); font-weight: 600; }
  /* THE PLACE'S OWN MARK, on the place page and in the map panel. It reads as
     a labelled row rather than a naked dropdown, because «This place» beside
     the states is the difference between a control and a stray select. */
  .placemark { margin: 16px 0 0; display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px;
    align-items: baseline; }
  .placemark-l { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 10px;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .05em; color: var(--ink-3); }
  :global(.at-placemark) { display: inline-flex; align-items: baseline; }
  :global(.at-placemark .srcmark) { margin-left: 0; }
  .ra-rollup, .ra-rollup-page { display: inline-block; margin-left: 10px;
    padding: 2px 8px; border-radius: 3px; background: var(--accent-wash);
    color: var(--ink-2); font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 11.5px; }
  .ra-rollup[hidden], .ra-rollup-page[hidden] { display: none; }
  #ra-mywork { margin: 10px 0 0; font-family: var(--sans); font-size: 12.5px;
    padding: 8px 11px; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent);
    background: var(--ground); border-radius: 0 3px 3px 0; }
  #ra-mywork[hidden] { display: none; }
</style>
